---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleCTA from '../../components/ArticleCTA.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'What Size USB Do DJs Actually Need?',
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Antonio Afonso' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Save My Gig!' },
};
---
<Base
  title="What Size USB for DJing? 32GB vs 64GB vs 128GB | Save My Gig!"
  description="How many tracks fit on a DJ USB, why bigger isn't safer on CDJs, and the size that professionals actually carry."
  path="/fix/best-usb-size-for-djing"
  ogImage="/og/best-usb-size-for-djing.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <div class="status-line">Drive_Size</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">HOW BIG SHOULD A<br>DJ <span class="accent">USB</span> BE?</h1>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>Short answer: 32-64GB, quality brand, and buy two.</strong> That's several thousand tracks, more music than you could play in a week of sets, with fast mounting and full FAT32 comfort.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:2rem 0 1rem">The track math</h2>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><strong>MP3 320kbps:</strong> ~12MB per track → a 32GB drive holds ~2,500 tracks; 64GB ~5,000.</li>
      <li><strong>Lossless (AIFF/WAV):</strong> ~40-60MB per track → 32GB ≈ 600 tracks, 64GB ≈ 1,200, still 20+ hours of music.</li>
      <li>A 4-hour set is 60-80 tracks. Even the lossless 32GB carries eight full sets of options.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:2rem 0 1rem">Why huge drives are worse in booths</h2>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><strong>Mount time:</strong> players index the database on insert, a stuffed 256GB drive can leave you staring at a loading screen mid-changeover.</li>
      <li><strong>Browsing:</strong> 20,000 tracks on a CDJ screen is not a library, it's a haystack. Curation beats capacity.</li>
      <li><strong>Failure blast radius:</strong> when the everything-drive dies, everything dies. Two medium drives beat one giant one - <a href="/fix/dj-usb-backup-strategy">the two-drive strategy →</a></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:2rem 0 1rem">What to actually buy</h2>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>32 or 64GB from a major brand, metal body preferred (booth drinks happen), USB-A: it works on every player. USB-C ports exist only on the CDJ-3000X generation onward.</li>
      <li>Format <span class="mono">FAT32 + MBR</span> regardless of what it ships with - <a href="/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj">here's why</a>.</li>
      <li>Two identical ones. Always two. <a href="/gear">The exact models we trust →</a></li>
    </ul>

    <ArticleCTA article="usb_size" />
  </main>
</Base>
